The FTP server
==============

"start ftp" accepts anonymous logins only.  Named logins are refused.


Why anonymous only
------------------

FTP over AX.25 or the AMPRNet is clear text.  A password sent to this server
is visible to anyone listening on the channel, so password authentication
here protects nothing while suggesting that it does.

The AX.25 login is a different matter and keeps working as before: there,
axserv_open() takes the callsign out of the frame header, so the identity is
a fact of the transmission.  An FTP user name is merely typed.  Answering
"230 User dl9sau logged in" to whoever types those nine characters produces a
log entry that looks like evidence and is not.

So access control lives in the file system, under the uid of the ftp account,
and the server does not pretend to know who is connecting.

There is history behind this.  The old password check could not work: on
Linux and 386BSD crypt() was #defined to return its first argument, which
turned the test into strcmp(cleartext, pw->pw_passwd).  When /etc/passwd
still held DES hashes that produced the intended policy - the auto-created
callsign accounts have an empty password field, so the check short-circuited
and let them in, while system accounts could never match.  Shadow passwords
inverted it: pw_passwd became "x", and from then on "PASS x" logged in as any
account, root included.  See SECURITY-AUDIT.md, finding W-35.


Setting it up
-------------

The server needs an account named "ftp".  Its home directory is the root of
the served tree; everything a client asks for is resolved beneath it.  If no
such account exists, every login is refused - that is the intended failure
mode, not a misconfiguration to work around.

Where to put the tree:

  Linux      /srv/ftp        FHS 3.0 defines /srv as "data for services
                             provided by this system".  /var/ftp is equally
                             reasonable on a Red Hat style box, where other
                             services already live under /var.
  macOS      there is no /srv.  /usr/local/wampes/ftp fits the default
             TCPDIR; under Homebrew, $(brew --prefix)/var/wampes/ftp.

Not /tmp: it is cleared on boot, it is world writable so any local user can
plant entries in it, and on many systems it is a tmpfs, where an upload eats
RAM instead of disk.

Give the ftp account a uid and gid of its own.  Not nobody, and not a gid
shared with anything else - seteugid(ftp->uid, ftp->gid) around every file
operation is what actually enforces the limits, so those numbers are the
whole security boundary.


Permissions
-----------

  /srv/ftp            root:root  755    the root itself must NOT be writable
                                        by the ftp uid, or a client can
                                        replace what is served from it
  /srv/ftp/pub        root:root  755    read-only material
  /srv/ftp/incoming   ftp:ftp    733    writable, deliberately not listable

733 on the upload directory is the classic drop box: a client can store a
file but cannot enumerate what is there, which keeps it from turning into a
public exchange for other people's content.

An anonymous client has STOR, APPE, DELE, MKD and RMD.  If you allow uploads
at all, put the tree on its own file system or set a quota, so that filling
it does not take the node down with it.


Two limits worth knowing
------------------------

The confinement is a prefix, not a chroot().  Paths are built as
ftp->root + file and pathname() collapses "..", so a client cannot climb out
by asking for one - that part is solid.  But fopen() and the "ls" that serves
LIST both follow symbolic links.  A symlink inside the tree that points
outside it will be followed.  Do not put any there.

The server runs as root and drops to the ftp uid only around the individual
file operations.  It is not a sandbox.


Named logins
------------

The code keeps two hooks for a future named-login path, compiled but unused:

  Ftp_allow_named_logins   master switch, 0
  Ftp_login_group          if set, e.g. "hams", the account's primary group
                           must match - which lets an operator lock someone
                           out by changing the gid in the passwd entry,
                           without touching the account

Turning the switch on is not by itself enough.  Such a path would have to
authenticate, and this file no longer knows how - deliberately.  Doing it
properly means delegating, the way login.c does when it execs /bin/login and
lets PAM decide, rather than comparing hashes here.  A root login stays
refused in any case: uid 0 or gid 0 is rejected before anything else.
